#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:37
msgid "Introduction to 3D in Godot"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:38
msgid "LET'S GET RIGHT INTO IT!"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:40
msgid "In this first tour, we will add the elements necessary for a 3D game and learn to navigate the 3D view."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:64
msgid "The unfinished game scene"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:67
msgid "I just opened the scene [b]unfinished_game.tscn[/b] for you."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:68
msgid "This small game scene has a character, platforms, and a flag."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:69
msgid "Run the scene by clicking the [b]Run Current Scene[/b] button at the top right of the editor or pressing [b]%s[/b] on your keyboard."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:70
msgid "You'll see a completely gray screen... Don't panic! It's to be expected. I'll explain why in the next step."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:71
msgid "Press [b]%s[/b] or close the game window to stop the game."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:78
msgid "The gray screen"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:81
msgid "When running the game, the screen is completely gray because the scene currently lacks a camera."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:82
msgid "In the 3D world, like in the real world, we need something like a pair of eyes or a virtual camera to tell the computer where to look and what to display."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:83
msgid "In Godot, to add and control the game's 3D camera, we use the [b]Camera3D[/b] node."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:88
msgid "Add the camera node"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:92
msgid "Let's add a [b]Camera3D[/b] node to the scene."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:93
msgid "To add the camera, select the [b]Game[/b] node in the Scene dock and click the [b]Add Child Node[/b] button at the top left. You can also press [b]%s+A[/b] on your keyboard to open the Add Node dialog. Search for the [b]Camera3D[/b] node and create it in the scene."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:96
msgid "This creates and places the camera at the origin of the 3D game world."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:97
msgid "Note that if you make a mistake at any point, you can press [b]%s+Z[/b] to undo the last action."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:102
msgid "Add a [b]Camera3D[/b] as a child of the [b]Game[/b] node"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:116
msgid "The Camera3D node"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:118
msgid "After creating the node, notice the camera icon in the 3D view."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:119
msgid "It comes with a small tapered wireframe box that extends from it. This wireframe represents the direction in which the camera is looking."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:128
#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:347
#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:518
msgid "Run the scene"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:131
msgid "Try running the scene now to see... something, at least! The camera is located at the world's origin, and it's facing a platform, so that's what you'll see."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:132
msgid "The running game remains dark because there are no lights in the scene. We will add a light later."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:133
msgid "Run the scene by clicking the [b]Run Current Scene[/b] button at the top right of the editor or pressing [b]%s[/b] on your keyboard. Press [b]%s[/b] or close the game window to stop the game."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:144
#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:291
msgid "The camera preview"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:147
msgid "At the top of the [b]Inspector[/b] dock on the right, you'll notice a camera preview. When you select the [b]Camera3D[/b] node, this preview shows exactly what the camera sees."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:148
msgid "The lighting in this preview might look different from your running scene. That's because the preview uses the editor's default lighting setup."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:149
msgid "We'll learn how to disable these default lights later in the tour. For now, let's focus on positioning the camera behind our character by moving the viewport and manipulating 3D nodes."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:157
msgid "How to navigate the 3D view"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:160
msgid "To move nodes in 3D, we often need to turn, pan, and zoom the view. Here's how you can do that in the 3D view:"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:162
msgid "To rotate the 3D view, hold the [b]Middle Mouse Button[/b] down and move the mouse."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:163
msgid "To pan the view and move around, hold the [b]Shift[/b] key and [b]Middle Mouse Button[/b] down, then move the mouse."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:164
msgid "To zoom in and out, use the mouse wheel."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:166
msgid "Try moving, turning, and zooming the view to test the controls."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:173
msgid "Focus the camera node"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:176
msgid "When you rotate the view, it rotates around a point. This point is the last point of [b]focus[/b] you set in the editor. By default, it's located at the center of the view."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:177
msgid "You can quickly align the view to a node and make that node the focus point by selecting a node and pressing the [b]%s[/b] key on your keyboard."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:178
msgid "Ensure that the [b]Camera3D[/b] node is selected in the [b]Scene[/b] dock and press [b]%s[/b] to focus the view on the camera."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:179
msgid "This will re-center the view on the camera. The view will rotate around this point until you change it again."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:180
msgid "Note that, for the shortcut to work, the mouse cursor should be over the 3D view."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:191
msgid "Rotate the view around the camera"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:193
msgid "Now that the camera node is in focus, try rotating the view. Hold the [b]Middle Mouse Button[/b] and drag to rotate the view around the camera."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:198
msgid "Select the Move Mode"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:201
msgid "To move a 3D object, as in 2D, you can use the %s [b]Move Mode[/b] in the toolbar at the top. Click the Move Mode icon in the toolbar above the viewport to display only the 3D position manipulator. You can also press [b]%s[/b] on your keyboard to activate it."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:207
msgid "Switch to [b]Move Mode[/b]"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:213
msgid "The 3D position manipulator"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:216
msgid "The position manipulator has three axes you can click and drag to move the camera node along that axis. The red line corresponds to the X axis, the green line to the vertical Y axis, and the blue line to the Z axis."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:217
msgid "You can also click and drag on the floating colored squares between axes to move the camera within the corresponding plane."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:224
msgid "Move the camera"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:227
msgid "Click and drag on the position manipulator axes and squares to move the camera node behind the character, in the floating blue box."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:228
msgid "Use the navigation tricks you learned previously to move the view as needed:"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:230
msgid "Change the editor's focus point with [b]%s[/b]."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:231
msgid "Rotate the view with the [b]Middle Mouse Click[/b]."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:232
msgid "Pan the view with [b]Shift + Middle Mouse Click[/b]."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:233
msgid "Zoom in and out with the [b]Mouse Wheel[/b]."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:243
msgid "The camera direction"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:246
msgid "Notice the tapered pink box that expands from the camera icon in the viewport. This box wireframe represents the direction in which the camera is looking."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:247
msgid "There's a little purple triangle above the purple wireframe in the viewport. It indicates the up direction of the camera. It should be pointing up or the view will be inverted."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:248
msgid "At the moment, the camera is looking away from the player character."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:249
msgid "To look at the player character, we need to rotate the box wireframe so that it extends towards the player."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:255
msgid "Select the Rotate mode"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:258
msgid "Select the %s [b]Rotate Mode[/b] in the toolbar above the viewport to display the rotation manipulator."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:259
msgid "This manipulator has three circles representing the rotations you can perform: you can turn the selected node around the [b]x[/b], [b]y[/b], and [b]z[/b] axes."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:260
msgid "For example, clicking and dragging on the green circle will rotate the camera around the vertical axis, the y-axis."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:265
#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:452
msgid "Turn the [b]Rotate Mode[/b] button ON."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:269
msgid "Make the camera look at the player"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:272
msgid "Using the Rotate mode, turn the camera to look towards the player. You can rotate it around the y axis by clicking and dragging the green circle on the rotation manipulator."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:273
msgid "Your camera should be behind the player character and looking at it. You don't need to make the orientation perfect; you'll be able to adjust it later."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:274
msgid "You can press [b]%s[/b] at any time to run the scene and see what the camera sees. Press [b]%s[/b] to stop the game."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:275
msgid "Don't worry about the fact everything is dark; we'll address that in a moment."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:282
msgid "Rotate the camera around the y axis to look at the player"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:294
msgid "While the [b]Inspector[/b] camera preview is helpful, it disappears as soon as you select any other node."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:295
msgid "Thankfully, Godot offers a better solution: the viewport preview. This lets you see through the camera's eyes while you work on your level."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:296
msgid "You can toggle the viewport preview by clicking the [b]Preview[/b] checkbox at the top left of the viewport."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:297
msgid "Once enabled, you should see your player character from the camera's perspective. You can also toggle this preview by pressing [b]%s+P[/b] on your keyboard."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:301
msgid "Turn on the camera preview"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:311
msgid "Split the view in two"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:314
msgid "With a single view, we have to switch in and out of preview to edit the scene. That's not convenient, is it?"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:315
msgid "We can split the view into two to see both simultaneously!"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:316
msgid "To split the view, click the [b]View[/b] button in the toolbar and select one of the options."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:321
msgid "Split the view into two vertical viewports"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:331
msgid "Working with two views"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:334
msgid "Notice how the view is now split into two viewports. The left viewport shows the camera preview, and the right viewport shows the free view."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:335
msgid "In the free view, you can move the camera around and see the scene from different angles."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:336
msgid "Try moving and turning the camera with the %s Move Mode (%s) and the %s Rotate Mode (%s) to see the camera preview update in real-time."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:350
msgid "Press [b]%s[/b] to play the scene again. You should see the player character and the platforms, with a pretty dark scene and a gray sky. Press [b]%s[/b] to close the running game."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:360
msgid "In 3D, you need light"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:363
msgid "A 3D game is composed of 3D geometry shaded by [b]materials[/b] and [b]light[/b]."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:364
msgid "Our scene has 3D geometry and materials predefined, but currently, it does not have light."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:365
msgid "It looks fine in the viewport because Godot provides default lighting to help us prototype scenes until we add our own lights."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:371
msgid "Turn off the default lights"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:374
msgid "To see the scene in the viewport as it looks in the game, we need to turn off the default lights."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:375
msgid "In the toolbar, click the %s [b]Toggle preview sunlight[/b] and %s [b]Toggle preview environment[/b] buttons to turn off the default lights."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:379
msgid "The viewport should become dark and gray, as in the game."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:387
msgid "Turn off the sun and environment preview"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:395
msgid "The most common light types"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:398
msgid "Godot has three nodes representing different types of lights:"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:400
msgid "The [b]OmniLight3D[/b] simulates light bulbs. It lights a spherical area."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:401
msgid "The [b]SpotLight3D[/b] simulates a flashlight or spotlight. It illuminates a cone-shaped area."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:402
msgid "The [b]DirectionalLight3D[/b] node simulates the sun. It applies uniform directional lighting to the entire scene."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:404
msgid "As our scene is an exterior, we will add a [b]DirectionalLight3D[/b] node to simulate sunlight."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:410
msgid "Add a directional light"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:413
msgid "Select the [b]Game[/b] node in the Scene dock and add a [b]DirectionalLight3D[/b] node as a child of it."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:414
msgid "You can press [b]%s+A[/b] on your keyboard to open the Add Node dialog. Search for the [b]DirectionalLight3D[/b] node and create it in the scene."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:418
msgid "Add a [b]DirectionalLight3D[/b] node to the scene"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:429
msgid "The directional light"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:432
msgid "At first, the light is aligned with the ground plane so it does not illuminate the floor. It illuminates the character from the front."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:433
msgid "We'll rotate the light to angle it down and light up the character and the ground."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:434
msgid "First, hover the right viewport with the mouse and focus the directional light by pressing the [b]%s[/b] key. This will center the view on the light."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:443
msgid "Activate the Rotate mode"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:446
msgid "We can use the [b]Rotate Mode[/b] in the toolbar to rotate the light. Click the icon or press [b]%s[/b] on your keyboard to activate the [b]Rotate Mode[/b]."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:457
msgid "The rotation manipulator"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:460
msgid "We used the rotation manipulator before with the camera. It has three circles representing the rotations you can perform: you can turn the selected node around the [b]x[/b], [b]y[/b], and [b]z[/b] axes."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:461
msgid "Left click and drag on any of these three circles to rotate the light around the corresponding axis."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:462
msgid "Left clicking and dragging anywhere else in the viewport will rotate the light parallel to the view's forward axis."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:468
msgid "Rotate the directional light"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:471
msgid "The light direction is represented by the white wire arrow that's aligned with the ground plane by default."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:472
msgid "Rotate the light by clicking and dragging on the red circle to rotate it around the [b]x[/b]-axis and angle it down."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:473
msgid "If you still have the two viewports active, you should see the character and the ground get lit uniformly from above."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:477
msgid "Rotate the light to angle it down"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:488
msgid "To shade or not to shade"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:491
msgid "Notice how the player character does not cast a shadow in the camera preview on the left."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:492
msgid "By default, lights in 3D games do not cast shadows because shadows have a rendering cost. Having too many shadow-casting lights can slow down the game, but having none doesn't give the visuals enough depth."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:493
msgid "Let's see how to activate shadows on our [b]DirectionalLight3D[/b] to make it cast shadows in the game."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:499
msgid "Enable shadows on the directional light"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:502
msgid "Make sure you have the [b]DirectionalLight3D[/b] node selected. Then, in the [b]Inspector[/b], turn on the [b]Enable[/b] property highlighted in the [b]Shadow[/b] category. Shadows will appear behind the character and the flag."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:503
msgid "There are a bunch of settings to tweak the quality and range of the shadows, but we'll learn all about that later in the course."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:507
msgid "Enable shadows on the [b]DirectionalLight3D[/b] node"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:521
msgid "Run the scene again with [b]%s[/b] to see that the character is lit and casts a shadow on the ground. The game should look slightly better now."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:522
msgid "Press [b]%s[/b] to close the running game."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:523
msgid "The scene still lacks a sky. We'll add one next."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:530
msgid "The world environment"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:533
msgid "While we added a directional light, we still have lots of shaded areas in our level, and the background is all gray."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:534
msgid "To control the background, we can use a [b]WorldEnvironment[/b] node. This node takes an [b]Environment[/b] resource and lets us define what the sky should look like. It also controls post-processing effects built into Godot, like fog, contrast, and more."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:535
msgid "We won't create an environment from scratch now; we'll learn how to do that later in the course. For now, we'll use one I've prepared for you."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:542
msgid "Add a WorldEnvironment node"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:545
msgid "Let's add a [b]WorldEnvironment[/b] node to the scene."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:546
msgid "Select the [b]Game[/b] node in the Scene dock and add a new [b]WorldEnvironment[/b] node as a child of it. This node alone does nothing; it needs an [b]Environment[/b] resource to work."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:547
msgid "We'll add it in the next step."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:551
msgid "Add a [b]WorldEnvironment[/b] node to the scene"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:563
msgid "Add the environment resource"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:566
msgid "With the [b]WorldEnvironment[/b] node selected, click and drag the [b]world_environment.tres[/b] file from the [b]FileSystem[/b] dock onto the [b]Environment[/b] property in the [b]Inspector[/b]."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:567
msgid "It's an environment I've prepared for you. It has a soft purple sky and uses the fog effect to give the platforms depth."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:577
msgid "Set the [b]Environment[/b] property to [b]%s[/b]"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:585
msgid "The ambient lighting"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:588
msgid "The sky brightens the scene and gives it a more appealing look. It uses a simple, uniform light that comes from all directions, called [b]ambient lighting[/b]."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:589
msgid "It's a very efficient and performance-friendly way to light a scene. It also works great for stylized visuals, like the style we're going for in this course."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:597
msgid "Expand the ambient light"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:600
msgid "Let's see how it works in the [b]Inspector[/b] dock. With the [b]WorldEnvironment[/b] node selected, in the Inspector, click on the [b]world_environment.tres[/b] resource to expand its properties. Then, expand the [b]Ambient Light[/b] category."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:601
msgid "It reveals four properties: [b]Source[/b], [b]Color[/b], [b]Sky Contribution[/b], and [b]Energy[/b]."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:602
msgid "The source of the ambient light is the purple sky, and it contributes to the scene's lighting. The sky colors are mixed with the [b]Color[/b] property based on the [b]Sky Contribution[/b] value. The [b]Energy[/b] property controls the intensity of the ambient light."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:609
msgid "Tweak the ambient light"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:612
msgid "Take a moment to play with these properties and see how they affect the scene."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:613
msgid "The [b]Color[/b] property is particularly fun to tweak! It tints all the platforms and the character with the color you choose."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:614
msgid "Note that the Color property won't do anything if [b]Sky Contribution[/b] is set to [b]1.0[/b]. You need to lower the sky's contribution to see the effect."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:624
msgid "Excellent!"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:625
msgid "CONTINUE"
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:628
msgid "That's it for this first tour! You've learned to navigate the 3D view, move and rotate nodes, and add light to a 3D scene."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:631
msgid "You've also seen how 3D games require a camera and light to work and look good. It's just like capturing a video in real life: you need a camera and lights."
msgstr ""

#: tours/103_introduction_to_3d/01_navigation_and_elements.gd:634
msgid "In the next few tours, we'll explore 3D geometry, materials, and the similarities and differences between 2D and 3D games."
msgstr ""
